<BaseLayout
  title="Install with your AI — Kvendra"
  description="Point your AI coding agent at kvendra.dev/llms.txt and have it install and configure Kvendra for you: bring up the self-hosted Platform, choose embeddings, wire up MCP, install Skills, and optionally add the CLI broker."
>
  <article class="kvd-prose">
    <span class="kvd-kicker">Install with your AI</span>
    <h1>Let your agent install Kvendra</h1>
    <p>
      Kvendra ships a machine-readable install guide following the
      <a href="https://llmstxt.org" rel="noreferrer">llms.txt</a> convention. It
      contains the whole setup — Platform up, embeddings, MCP wiring, Skills, and
      the optional CLI — condensed into unambiguous, copy-paste steps, in order,
      with a verification after each one. An agent can read it and run the setup
      end-to-end.
    </p>

    <p>Point your Claude Code at the guide and ask:</p>
    <blockquote>
      Read https://kvendra.dev/llms.txt and install and configure Kvendra for
      me.
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      You can also read it yourself: <a href="/llms.txt">kvendra.dev/llms.txt</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2>What the agent will do</h2>
    <p>
      The guide is platform-first, so the agent starts with the one required
      piece — the self-hosted knowledge base — and layers the rest on top:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Check prerequisites.</strong> Docker with the daemon running,
        Docker Compose v2, git and curl — and it tells you exactly what to
        install if one is missing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Bring up the Platform.</strong> Reuse a reference-stack clone
        you already have, or clone it, start it with <code>up.sh</code>, and
        confirm the health check on port <code>7777</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Choose embeddings.</strong> Ollama as a local container
        (default, nothing leaves your machine), your own OpenAI-compatible
        embeddings provider, or a mock provider for CI.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Connect Claude Code over MCP.</strong> Read the token, register
        the Platform with <code>claude mcp add --transport http</code>, ask you
        to restart Claude Code once, and prove the connection with a real read
        — which gives the agent the 14 knowledge-base tools.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Install the Skills and onboard.</strong> Add the Claude Code
        plugin, onboard your project with
        <code>/kvendra-skills:onboard-project</code>, and from then on work
        with <code>/kvendra-skills:kvendra</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Optionally add the CLI broker.</strong> A zero-knowledge vault
        and capability broker for brokered operations — optional and pre-alpha.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Honest about where this is</h2>
    <p>
      The Platform and Skills are the parts you run today. The CLI broker is
      <strong>optional and pre-alpha</strong> — Skills and the Platform work
      without it, and the guide marks it clearly so your agent does not treat it
      as required. Everything the guide references is open-core: the Platform is
      AGPL, the CLI is Apache-2.0, the Skills are MIT, and the reference stack is
      MIT.
    </p>

    <p>
      Prefer to follow along by hand? The same steps live in the
      <a href="/docs/getting-started/">Quickstart</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
